IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `clact-dates`

-- Limitation Act 1985 (ACT), Division 4.4 (choice of law) and the two choice-of-law sections beside it (s 54, s 21BC (3)
-- (b)) -- the questions of the person bringing a claim.
--
-- SEAT: the claimant: the person who brings a claim before a court of the Australian Capital Territory or an
-- arbitrator, where the claim is governed by the law of an Australian State, another Territory or New Zealand (an injury
-- suffered in New South Wales and sued on in Canberra, a contract governed by Victorian law), or who sues another State
-- or Territory in the ACT to get back a tax or duty. "Is this time rule a 'limitation law'?", "Whose time limit does the
-- court apply to my claim?", "Am I in time?", "If I ask for more time, how must the court decide?", "Can I still sue
-- that State for the tax back?". The court and the arbitrator get no seat: what they would find is asked as a fact.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT. The
-- one duty with a deadline (to commence within the time another place's limitation law allows, which s 56 makes part of
-- the law the court applies, and which s 54 applies to a revenue action against another State or Territory) is a
-- regulative rule run inside a question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: Division 4.4 whole: s 55 (definitions: court, limitation law), s 56 (characterisation of
-- limitation laws), s 57 (exercise of discretion under limitation law); s 54 (recovery of State or Territory revenue
-- amounts); s 21BC (3) (b) (the electronic "first uploaded" rule does not decide the choice of law for defamation). The
-- rest of the Act (Parts 1 to 3, Divisions 4.1 to 4.3 apart from s 54, Division 4.5) is out: NOTES.md.
--
-- This Division is the ACT's counterpart of the Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Acts of the States and the Northern
-- Territory; the ACT has no separate Act of that name.
--
-- Edition: Limitation Act 1985 (ACT), A1985-66, Republication No 28 (R28), effective 26 November 2025, deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/A1985-66.txt. Division 4.4 and s 54 were inserted by the Limitation (Amendment) Act
-- 1993 A1993-82 s 6, commenced 30 November 1993. No regulation sets a number this encoding needs.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to a court are INPUTS ("if the courts of that
-- place would ..."). Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that is
-- the text's own no is marked "(the text's no)"; an answer where the text is limited by its own wording, or silent, is
-- marked "NOTHING-kind".

§ `Limitation Act 1985 (ACT), choice of law: the questions of the person bringing a claim`

-- s 55: "court includes arbitrator". ss 56 and 57 speak of "a court of the Territory". Fork F2: an arbitrator is taken
-- to be within "a court of the Territory" when the arbitration is held in the ACT.
DECLARE `Where my claim is heard` IS ONE OF
    `a court of the Australian Capital Territory`
    `an arbitrator, in an arbitration held in the Australian Capital Territory`
    `a court or arbitrator outside the Australian Capital Territory`

-- ss 56 and 57: "another place being a State, another Territory or New Zealand". Which law governs a claim is decided by
-- the common law of choice of law, which the Act does not state: it is asked as a fact. "another Territory": any
-- Territory other than the ACT, internal (the Northern Territory, the Jervis Bay Territory) or external (Norfolk Island
-- and the others) -- fork F5.
DECLARE `The law that governs my claim` IS ONE OF
    `the law of the Australian Capital Territory`
    `the law of an Australian State`
    `the law of another Territory`
    `the law of New Zealand`
    `the law of a country other than Australia or New Zealand`

GIVEN `the law` IS A `The law that governs my claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a State, another Territory or New Zealand` `the law` IF
        `the law` EQUALS `the law of an Australian State`
    OR  `the law` EQUALS `the law of another Territory`
    OR  `the law` EQUALS `the law of New Zealand`

GIVEN `the forum` IS A `Where my claim is heard`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a court of the Territory` `the forum` IF
        `the forum` EQUALS `a court of the Australian Capital Territory`
    OR  `the forum` EQUALS `an arbitrator, in an arbitration held in the Australian Capital Territory`

§§ `s 55 -- Definitions for div 4.4`

-- s 55: "limitation law means a law that provides for the limitation or exclusion of any liability or the barring of a
-- right of action in relation to a claim by reference to the time when a proceeding on, or the arbitration of, the claim
-- is begun." A "means" definition: when either element fails, the answer is the text's no (FALSE).
-- Plain gloss: a time limit for suing, whether it kills the claim, the remedy or the liability.
@export Is the time rule I face a "limitation law" within the Limitation Act 1985 (ACT), Division 4.4 (s 55)? A limitation law is a law that limits or excludes a liability, or bars a right of action, on a claim by reference to the time when a proceeding on the claim, or its arbitration, is begun.
GIVEN `limits or bars` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the rule limits or excludes a liability, or bars a right of action, on your claim (for example, says no action may be brought, or that the right is extinguished)
      `by the time of beginning` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it does so by reference to the time when a proceeding on the claim, or the arbitration of the claim, is begun (for example, "within 3 years after the cause of action accrued")
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 55 -- is it a limitation law` `limits or bars` `by the time of beginning` IF
        `limits or bars`
    AND `by the time of beginning`

§§ `s 56 -- Characterisation of limitation laws`

-- s 56: "If the substantive law of another place being a State, another Territory or New Zealand, is to govern a claim
-- before a court of the Territory, a limitation law of that place is to be regarded as part of that substantive law and
-- applied accordingly by the court."
-- The "if" failing is the text's no (ruling (b) of 2026-11-05, "if" read as "where"): s 56 does not make the other
-- place's limitation law apply. What applies instead is not said in s 56 (fork F6): where ACT law governs, the answer
-- points to the Act's own periods (Parts 2 and 3), which apply to "an action" on their own terms; for a claim governed
-- by the law of another country, the Act is silent (NOTHING-kind).
DECLARE `What s 56 says about the time limit on my claim` IS ONE OF
    `the limitation law of the place whose law governs the claim applies to it, as part of that place's substantive law (s 56)`
    `s 56 does not apply: the claim is not before a court or arbitrator of the Territory (the text's no: its "if" fails); this Act does not say which time limit applies`
    `s 56 does not apply: ACT law governs the claim (the text's no: its "if" fails); the time limits are this Act's own, in Parts 2 and 3 (not encoded here)`
    `s 56 does not apply: the law of a country other than Australia or New Zealand governs the claim (the text's no: its "if" fails); this Act does not say which time limit applies (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Whose time limit applies to my claim before an ACT court or arbitrator (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 56)? If the law of an Australian State, another Territory or New Zealand governs the claim, that place's limitation law is treated as part of its substantive law and the court applies it, not the ACT's.
GIVEN `the forum` IS A `Where my claim is heard` @desc Where your claim is (or will be) heard
      `the law` IS A `The law that governs my claim` @desc Which place's law governs your claim (its substantive law, as a court would decide under the rules of choice of law)
GIVETH A `What s 56 says about the time limit on my claim`
`s 56 -- whose time limit applies to my claim` `the forum` `the law` MEANS
    IF NOT (`a court of the Territory` `the forum`)
    THEN `s 56 does not apply: the claim is not before a court or arbitrator of the Territory (the text's no: its "if" fails); this Act does not say which time limit applies`
    ELSE IF `the law` EQUALS `the law of the Australian Capital Territory`
         THEN `s 56 does not apply: ACT law governs the claim (the text's no: its "if" fails); the time limits are this Act's own, in Parts 2 and 3 (not encoded here)`
         ELSE IF NOT (`a State, another Territory or New Zealand` `the law`)
              THEN `s 56 does not apply: the law of a country other than Australia or New Zealand governs the claim (the text's no: its "if" fails); this Act does not say which time limit applies (NOTHING-kind)`
              ELSE `the limitation law of the place whose law governs the claim applies to it, as part of that place's substantive law (s 56)`

§§ `Beginning within the time another place's limitation law allows (s 56, s 54)`

-- The claimant's duty with a deadline. Division 4.4 imposes no time limit of its own; s 56 makes the governing place's
-- limitation law part of the law the court applies, and s 54 bars a revenue action against another State or Territory
-- once that place's period has ended. Either way the claimant must begin within the time that law allows. The deadline
-- is counted in days from the day time began to run under that law to the last day that law allows; both days are the
-- claimant's facts, read from that law (its counting rules are not this Act's: fork F3).
DECLARE `Claimant` IS ONE OF `the claimant`
DECLARE `Claim step` IS ONE OF `begin the proceeding or arbitration`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claimant` `Claim step`
`begin within the time the other place's limitation law allows` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the claimant`
    MUST `begin the proceeding or arbitration`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "not begun by the last day the other place's limitation law allows: s 56 makes that law part of the law the court applies, and s 54 bars a revenue action once that period has ended"

-- Runs the rule over what happened. FULFILLED means in time and anything else late. It takes no DEONTIC from outside
-- and is not exported.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `beginning on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`begin within the time the other place's limitation law allows` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the claimant` `begin the proceeding or arbitration` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- Whether a proceeding begun (or, if not yet begun, still to be begun) is in time, given the day time began, the last
-- day, and today. A proceeding begun before time began to run is counted as begun on day 0 (in time). Fork F7.
DECLARE `Timing` IS ONE OF
    `begun in time`
    `not yet begun, still in time`
    `out of time`

GIVEN `begun` IS A BOOLEAN, `begun on` IS A DATE, `today` IS A DATE, `time began on` IS A DATE, `last day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Timing`
`the timing` `begun` `begun on` `today` `time began on` `last day` MEANS
    IF `begun`
    THEN IF `beginning on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day begun`
         THEN `begun in time`
         ELSE `out of time`
    ELSE IF `last day` `falls before` `today`
         THEN `out of time`
         ELSE `not yet begun, still in time`
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `time began on` `to` `last day`
        `the raw day` MEANS `the days from` `time began on` `to` `begun on`
        `the day begun` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`

DECLARE `Am I in time` IS ONE OF
    `in time: you began on or before the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows (s 56)`
    `still in time: you have not begun, and the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows has not passed (s 56)`
    `out of time under the limitation law of the governing place (s 56): only a discretion that law gives the court can help (see s 57)`
    `s 56 does not make another place's time limit apply on these facts (the text's no)`

@export Am I in time under the limitation law that the ACT court must apply to my claim (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 56)? Where s 56 applies, you must begin the proceeding or arbitration by the last day the limitation law of the governing State, Territory or New Zealand allows.
GIVEN `the forum` IS A `Where my claim is heard` @desc Where your claim is (or will be) heard
      `the law` IS A `The law that governs my claim` @desc Which place's law governs your claim (its substantive law, as a court would decide under the rules of choice of law)
      `begun` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already begun proceedings on the claim (filed in court, or begun the arbitration)
      `begun on` IS A DATE @desc The day you began them (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not yet begun proceedings)
      `time began on` IS A DATE @desc The day time began to run against you under the governing place's limitation law (usually when the cause of action accrued)
      `last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day on which that limitation law lets you begin the proceeding or arbitration
GIVETH A `Am I in time`
`s 56 -- am I in time` `the forum` `the law` `begun` `begun on` `today` `time began on` `last day` MEANS
    IF NOT (`s 56 -- whose time limit applies to my claim` `the forum` `the law`
                EQUALS `the limitation law of the place whose law governs the claim applies to it, as part of that place's substantive law (s 56)`)
    THEN `s 56 does not make another place's time limit apply on these facts (the text's no)`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the timing` `begun` `begun on` `today` `time began on` `last day`
         WHEN `begun in time` THEN `in time: you began on or before the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows (s 56)`
         WHEN `not yet begun, still in time` THEN `still in time: you have not begun, and the last day the limitation law of the governing place allows has not passed (s 56)`
         WHEN `out of time` THEN `out of time under the limitation law of the governing place (s 56): only a discretion that law gives the court can help (see s 57)`

§§ `s 57 -- Exercise of discretion under limitation law`

-- s 57: "If a court of the Territory exercises a discretion given under a limitation law of a place being a State,
-- another Territory or New Zealand that discretion, as far as practicable, is to be exercised in the way in which it is
-- exercised in comparable cases by the courts of that place."
-- The "if" failing is the text's no (ruling (b) of 2026-11-05). "As far as practicable" is a limit in the section's own
-- wording: where following that place's courts is not practicable, s 57 says no more (NOTHING-kind, fork F8). How the
-- courts of that place exercise the discretion in comparable cases is a judgment, asked as a fact.
DECLARE `How the court must use a discretion under that limitation law` IS ONE OF
    `as the courts of that place do in comparable cases, and on these facts they would exercise it in your favour (s 57)`
    `as the courts of that place do in comparable cases, and on these facts they would not exercise it in your favour (s 57)`
    `s 57 asks the court to follow that place's courts only as far as practicable; here it is not practicable, and s 57 says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 57 does not arise: that limitation law gives the court no discretion that you have asked it to exercise (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `s 57 does not arise: not a court or arbitrator of the Territory, or not the limitation law of a State, another Territory or New Zealand (the text's no: its "if" fails)`

@export If I ask the ACT court to use a discretion under another place's limitation law (for example, to extend time), how must it decide (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 57)? As far as practicable, the court exercises the discretion as the courts of that State, Territory or New Zealand exercise it in comparable cases.
GIVEN `the forum` IS A `Where my claim is heard` @desc Where your claim is (or will be) heard
      `the law` IS A `The law that governs my claim` @desc The place whose limitation law gives the discretion (usually the place whose law governs your claim)
      `a discretion asked for` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that place's limitation law gives the court a discretion (for example, to extend the limitation period) and you have asked the court to exercise it
      `practicable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is practicable for the court to follow how the courts of that place exercise the discretion (for example, whether there are comparable cases from those courts to follow)
      `that place's courts would favour me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the courts of that place, in cases comparable to yours, would exercise the discretion in your favour
GIVETH A `How the court must use a discretion under that limitation law`
`s 57 -- how must the court use a discretion under that limitation law` `the forum` `the law` `a discretion asked for` `practicable` `that place's courts would favour me` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`a court of the Territory` `the forum`)) OR (NOT (`a State, another Territory or New Zealand` `the law`))
    THEN `s 57 does not arise: not a court or arbitrator of the Territory, or not the limitation law of a State, another Territory or New Zealand (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    ELSE IF NOT `a discretion asked for`
         THEN `s 57 does not arise: that limitation law gives the court no discretion that you have asked it to exercise (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
         ELSE IF NOT `practicable`
              THEN `s 57 asks the court to follow that place's courts only as far as practicable; here it is not practicable, and s 57 says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
              ELSE IF `that place's courts would favour me`
                   THEN `as the courts of that place do in comparable cases, and on these facts they would exercise it in your favour (s 57)`
                   ELSE `as the courts of that place do in comparable cases, and on these facts they would not exercise it in your favour (s 57)`

§§ `s 54 -- Recovery of State or Territory revenue amounts`

-- s 54 (1): "An action against a State or another Territory for recovery of a revenue amount imposed, or purportedly
-- imposed, under a law of the State or Territory is not maintainable if the relevant limitation period of the State or
-- Territory has ended."
-- s 54 (3): "a reference to an action against a State or another Territory includes a reference to an action against
-- an officer, a Minister or an authority of the State or Territory."
-- s 54 (2): "relevant limitation period ... means the limitation period that would apply to an action for recovery of a
-- revenue amount if the action were brought in the State or Territory." Its last day is the claimant's fact (fork F3).
-- Plain gloss: if you paid another State's tax that turns out to be invalid and sue for it in the ACT, the ACT court
-- holds you to that State's own time limit for such a claim.

-- Who the action is against.
DECLARE `Who I am suing` IS ONE OF
    `an Australian State, or another Territory`
    `an officer, a Minister or an authority of an Australian State or another Territory`
    `the Australian Capital Territory, or its officers or authorities`
    `someone else (the Commonwealth, a person or a company)`

-- What the money was paid as. s 54 (2) "revenue amount" (a), (b).
DECLARE `What I paid the money as` IS ONE OF
    `a tax, licence fee or duty imposed, or purportedly imposed, under a law of that State or Territory`
    `penalty tax on such a tax, licence fee or duty`
    `something else`

-- s 54 (2): "revenue amount means an amount of money paid voluntarily or under compulsion as (a) a tax, licence fee or
-- duty imposed, or purportedly imposed, under a law; or (b) penalty tax in relation to such a tax, licence fee or duty;
-- if the amount would have been legally owing if the provision under which it was paid had been valid."
-- A "means" definition with an "if": when an element fails, the text's no. "voluntarily or under compulsion" covers
-- every payment, so it is not asked. s 54 (1) adds that the amount was imposed under a law "of the State or Territory"
-- sued; the choice above says so.
@export Is the money I want back a "revenue amount" within the Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 54 (2)? It is money paid (voluntarily or under compulsion) as a tax, licence fee or duty imposed or purportedly imposed under a law, or penalty tax on one, where it would have been legally owing if the provision under which it was paid had been valid.
GIVEN `paid as` IS A `What I paid the money as` @desc What you paid the money as
      `owing if valid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the amount would have been legally owing if the provision under which you paid it had been valid (that is, your complaint is that the provision is invalid, not that you were charged the wrong amount under it)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 54 -- is it a revenue amount` `paid as` `owing if valid` IF
        (    `paid as` EQUALS `a tax, licence fee or duty imposed, or purportedly imposed, under a law of that State or Territory`
         OR  `paid as` EQUALS `penalty tax on such a tax, licence fee or duty`)
    AND `owing if valid`

DECLARE `Can I still sue for the revenue amount` IS ONE OF
    `yes, as far as s 54 goes: you began before that State's or Territory's relevant limitation period ended (s 54 (1) does not bar you)`
    `yes, as far as s 54 goes: you have not begun, and that State's or Territory's relevant limitation period has not ended (begin by its last day)`
    `no: that State's or Territory's relevant limitation period has ended, so the action is not maintainable (s 54 (1))`
    `s 54 does not apply: the money is not a revenue amount (s 54 (2), a "means" definition: the text's no)`
    `s 54 does not apply: the action is not against another State or Territory, or its officer, Minister or authority (NOTHING-kind: s 54 says nothing about other defendants)`

@export Can I still sue another State or Territory in the ACT to get back a tax, licence fee or duty paid under its law (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 54)? The action is not maintainable once the limitation period that State or Territory would apply to such an action has ended.
GIVEN `the defendant` IS A `Who I am suing` @desc Who your action to recover the money is (or will be) against
      `paid as` IS A `What I paid the money as` @desc What you paid the money as
      `owing if valid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the amount would have been legally owing if the provision under which you paid it had been valid (that is, your complaint is that the provision is invalid)
      `begun` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already begun the action
      `begun on` IS A DATE @desc The day you began it (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (used when you have not yet begun the action)
      `time began on` IS A DATE @desc The day time began to run against you under that State's or Territory's limitation period for an action to recover such an amount (for example, the day you paid)
      `last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the limitation period that would apply if the action were brought in that State or Territory
GIVETH A `Can I still sue for the revenue amount`
`s 54 -- can I still sue for the revenue amount` `the defendant` `paid as` `owing if valid` `begun` `begun on` `today` `time began on` `last day` MEANS
    IF NOT (    `the defendant` EQUALS `an Australian State, or another Territory`
            OR  `the defendant` EQUALS `an officer, a Minister or an authority of an Australian State or another Territory`)
    THEN `s 54 does not apply: the action is not against another State or Territory, or its officer, Minister or authority (NOTHING-kind: s 54 says nothing about other defendants)`
    ELSE IF NOT (`s 54 -- is it a revenue amount` `paid as` `owing if valid`)
         THEN `s 54 does not apply: the money is not a revenue amount (s 54 (2), a "means" definition: the text's no)`
         ELSE CONSIDER `the timing` `begun` `begun on` `today` `time began on` `last day`
              WHEN `begun in time` THEN `yes, as far as s 54 goes: you began before that State's or Territory's relevant limitation period ended (s 54 (1) does not bar you)`
              WHEN `not yet begun, still in time` THEN `yes, as far as s 54 goes: you have not begun, and that State's or Territory's relevant limitation period has not ended (begin by its last day)`
              WHEN `out of time` THEN `no: that State's or Territory's relevant limitation period has ended, so the action is not maintainable (s 54 (1))`

§§ `s 21BC (3) (b) -- Electronic publication and the choice of law for defamation`

-- s 21BC (1)-(3): the rule in ss 21B and 21BA that the date of publication of matter in electronic form is "the day on
-- which the matter was first uploaded for access or sent electronically to a recipient" is "relevant only for the
-- purpose of determining when a limitation period begins and for no other purpose", and "is not relevant for ... (b) the
-- choice of law to be applied for a cause of action for defamation."
-- A flat negative: the text's no. For matter not in electronic form s 21BC does not apply (its (1) "applies in relation
-- to" the electronic rule only): NOTHING-kind.
DECLARE `Does the first-upload day decide the choice of law` IS ONE OF
    `no: the first-upload day counts only for when the limitation period begins, not for which place's law governs your defamation claim (s 21BC (2), (3) (b), the text's no)`
    `s 21BC does not apply: the matter was not published in electronic form (NOTHING-kind: s 21BC speaks only of the electronic rule)`

@export Does the day the matter was first uploaded or sent electronically decide which place's law governs my defamation claim (Limitation Act 1985 (ACT) s 21BC (3) (b))? No: that day counts only for when the limitation period begins.
GIVEN `electronic` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter you complain of was published in electronic form (uploaded for access or sent electronically)
GIVETH A `Does the first-upload day decide the choice of law`
`s 21BC -- does the first-upload day decide the choice of law` `electronic` MEANS
    IF `electronic`
    THEN `no: the first-upload day counts only for when the limitation period begins, not for which place's law governs your defamation claim (s 21BC (2), (3) (b), the text's no)`
    ELSE `s 21BC does not apply: the matter was not published in electronic form (NOTHING-kind: s 21BC speaks only of the electronic rule)`
